        <p>Chart week is the Billboard Hot 100 week that contains November 12 that year.</p>
        <p>Week of run says which week of the song's time at number one that was, like 5 of 10.</p>
        <p>Chart weeks and weeks at number one come from this site's weekly Hot 100 file, and the story lines are credited in Sources.</p>

        <h2 id="answer">The Short Answer: Who Was on Top Each Year</h2>
        <p>For November 12 in music history, a different record was number one in every year from 1970 to 1979.</p>
        <p>That is the Hot 100 chart week that includes November 12.</p>
        <p>Here is what the ten weeks show at a glance:</p>
        <ul>
          <li>The ten songs spent {{TOTAL_WEEKS}} weeks at number one in total.</li>
          <li>"You Light Up My Life" had the most weeks, with ten.</li>
          <li>"I'll Be There" came next with five, and "I Can See Clearly Now" had four.</li>
          <li>"Island Girl" and "MacArthur Park" had three each, and two songs had two.</li>
          <li>Three songs held number one for a single week.</li>
          <li>{{N_FIRST}} of the ten songs were in the first week of their run, and {{N_MID}} were in the middle.</li>
          <li>None of the ten was in its last week on top.</li>
        </ul>

        <h3>Want Your Own Week?</h3>
        <p>The <a href="/pages/birthday-number-one">Birthday #1 Song Finder</a> does this for any 1970s birthday, not only November 12 in music history.</p>
        <p>Enter the date and it shows the song that was on top that week.</p>

        <h2 id="method">How the November 12 Week Was Picked</h2>
        <p>Billboard chart weeks do not line up with the calendar, so there is no single week called November 12.</p>
        <p>This November 12 in music history page uses the weekly Hot 100 week that contains November 12 each year.</p>
        <p>In 1977 that week began on November 12 itself, and in the other nine years it began a few days before:</p>
        <ul>
{{DATE_LIST}}
        </ul>

        <h3>Where the Numbers Come From</h3>
        <p>Chart weeks and weeks at number one come from the weekly Hot 100 file on this site.</p>
        <p>The story line for each song is a fact from that song's Wikipedia page.</p>
        <p>Anything that could not be confirmed against those sources was left out.</p>
        <p>All ten runs were unbroken.</p>
        <p>So "week of run" counts the weeks since the song first reached number one.</p>

        <h3>Six Songs Also on the November 7 List</h3>
        <p>This is the real week of November 12, so every song on top that week is here.</p>
        <p>Six of them are also on the <a href="/blog/trivia/november-7-in-music-history/">November 7 in music history</a> list.</p>
        <p>In 1971 and 1976 both dates fell in the same chart week.</p>
        <p>In 1970, 1972, 1975 and 1977 a longer run carried the same song across both weeks.</p>
        <p>The other four years had a new number one by November 12.</p>

        <h2 id="early">November 12 in Music History, 1970 to 1972: Hitsville West, the Wrecking Crew and a Reggae Lilt</h2>
        <p>November 12 in music history opened the decade with a Jackson 5 ballad near the end of its run.</p>
        <p>Cher followed in her first week on top.</p>
        <p>Then came Johnny Nash, one week into a four-week stay.</p>

        <h3>1970: "I'll Be There" by The Jackson 5</h3>
        <p>Berry Gordy, Hal Davis, Bob West and Willie Hutch wrote "I'll Be There."</p>
        <p>Wikipedia says the group recorded it in June 1970 at Hitsville West in Los Angeles.</p>
        <p>Motown released it on August 28, 1970, as the first single from Third Album.</p>
        <p>It was also number one on the soul singles chart for six weeks.</p>
        <p>Wikipedia puts its sales at 4.2 million in the United States, and it entered the Grammy Hall of Fame in 2011.</p>
        <p>This site's weekly data shows five weeks at number one, from October 17 to November 14, 1970.</p>
        <p>Neil Diamond's "Cracklin' Rosie" was on top before it, and The Partridge Family's "I Think I Love You" came next.</p>
        <p>So the chart week that contains November 12 was the fourth of five.</p>
        <p>The song is number 7 on the 1970 year-end Hot 100, in this site's year-end data.</p>
        <p>The full story is on the <a href="/blog/songs/ill-be-there-jackson-5/">I'll Be There</a> page.</p>

        <h3>1971: "Gypsys, Tramps &amp; Thieves" by Cher</h3>
        <p>Bob Stone wrote "Gypsys, Tramps &amp; Thieves," and Snuff Garrett produced it.</p>
        <p>Wikipedia says L.A. session musicians from the Wrecking Crew played on it.</p>
        <p>Kapp Records released it on September 1, 1971, as the lead single from the album Ch&eacute;r.</p>
        <p>It was the biggest-selling single in MCA's history at the time.</p>
        <p>This site's weekly data shows two weeks at number one, from November 6 to November 13, 1971.</p>
        <p>Rod Stewart's "Maggie May" was on top before it, and Isaac Hayes's "Theme from Shaft" came next.</p>
        <p>So the chart week that contains November 12 was the first of two.</p>
        <p>The song is number 39 on the 1971 year-end Hot 100, in this site's year-end data.</p>
        <p>More solo stars of the decade are on the <a href="/blog/artists/70s-female-singers/">70s female singers</a> page.</p>

        <h3>1972: "I Can See Clearly Now" by Johnny Nash</h3>
        <p>Johnny Nash wrote "I Can See Clearly Now" and recorded it in London.</p>
        <p>Wikipedia says its arrangement and style are heavily laced with reggae influences.</p>
        <p>It was the lead single from his twelfth album.</p>
        <p>Hothouse Flowers released their own version in 1990.</p>
        <p>This site's weekly data shows four weeks at number one, from November 4 to November 25, 1972.</p>
        <p>Chuck Berry's "My Ding-a-Ling" was on top before it, and The Temptations' "Papa Was a Rollin' Stone" came next.</p>
        <p>So the chart week that contains November 12 was the second of four.</p>
        <p>The song is number 47 on the 1972 year-end Hot 100, in this site's year-end data.</p>

        <h2 id="middle">November 12 in Music History, 1973 to 1975: A Former Temptation, a Stutter and an Elton John Climb</h2>
        <p>November 12 in music history in the middle of the decade brought two brand-new number ones.</p>
        <p>Eddie Kendricks and Bachman-Turner Overdrive each reached the top that week.</p>
        <p>Elton John was one week into a three-week run.</p>

        <h3>1973: "Keep on Truckin'" by Eddie Kendricks</h3>
        <p>Frank Wilson, Anita Poree and Leonard Caston Jr. wrote "Keep on Truckin'."</p>
        <p>Wikipedia says Wilson was the main producer on most of Kendricks' solo records.</p>
        <p>The song came out in August 1973, two years after Kendricks left the Temptations.</p>
        <p>It reached number one on both the Hot 100 and the R&amp;B singles chart.</p>
        <p>It was his only number one solo hit, and it reached number 18 in the UK.</p>
        <p>This site's weekly data shows two weeks at number one, from November 10 to November 17, 1973.</p>
        <p>Gladys Knight &amp; the Pips' "Midnight Train to Georgia" was on top before it, and Ringo Starr's "Photograph" came next.</p>
        <p>So the chart week that contains November 12 was the first of two.</p>
        <p>The song is number 35 on the 1973 year-end Hot 100, in this site's year-end data.</p>
        <p>More of that sound is in <a href="/blog/genres/70s-soul-music/">70s soul music</a>.</p>

        <h3>1974: "You Ain't Seen Nothing Yet" by Bachman-Turner Overdrive</h3>
        <p>Randy Bachman wrote "You Ain't Seen Nothing Yet" for the band's third studio album, Not Fragile.</p>
        <p>Wikipedia says he sang it with a stutter as a joke for his brother Gary, who had a stutter.</p>
        <p>They meant to record it only once and send that one copy to Gary.</p>
        <p>The single came out in September 1974.</p>
        <p>It also went to number one in Canada and won the 1976 Juno Award for best-selling single.</p>
        <p>In the UK it stopped at number two, behind Mud's "Lonely This Christmas."</p>
        <p>This site's weekly data shows one week at number one, the chart week of November 9, 1974.</p>
        <p>Stevie Wonder's "You Haven't Done Nothin'" was on top before it, and John Lennon's "Whatever Gets You thru the Night" came next.</p>
        <p>The song is number 98 on the 1975 year-end Hot 100, in this site's year-end data.</p>
        <p>Louder records of the decade are on the <a href="/blog/genres/70s-hard-rock/">70s hard rock</a> list.</p>

        <h3>1975: "Island Girl" by Elton John</h3>
        <p>Elton John wrote the music for "Island Girl," and Bernie Taupin wrote the lyrics.</p>
        <p>Gus Dudgeon produced it, and it was recorded in June and July 1975.</p>
        <p>It came out on September 29, 1975, as the first single from Rock of the Westies.</p>
        <p>Wikipedia says it entered the Hot 100 at number 49 on October 11, 1975, and reached the top in four weeks.</p>
        <p>The RIAA certified it Platinum.</p>
        <p>This site's weekly data shows three weeks at number one, from November 1 to November 15, 1975.</p>
        <p>Neil Sedaka's "Bad Blood" was on top before it, and KC and the Sunshine Band's "That's the Way (I Like It)" came next.</p>
        <p>So the chart week that contains November 12 was the second of three.</p>
        <p>The song is number 65 on the 1976 year-end Hot 100, in this site's year-end data.</p>
        <p>More of his records are on the <a href="/blog/artists/elton-john/">Elton John</a> page.</p>

        <h2 id="late">November 12 in Music History, 1976 to 1979: Steve Miller, a Ten-Week Ballad, Donna Summer and the Eagles</h2>
        <p>November 12 in music history closed the decade with three songs in their first week on top.</p>
        <p>Steve Miller, Donna Summer and the Eagles each reached number one that week.</p>
        <p>In between, Debby Boone was halfway through a ten-week run.</p>

        <h3>1976: "Rock'n Me" by Steve Miller Band</h3>
        <p>Steve Miller wrote and produced "Rock'n Me."</p>
        <p>Wikipedia says it was recorded in 1976 at CBS studios in San Francisco.</p>
        <p>It came out in August 1976 as the second single from Fly Like an Eagle.</p>
        <p>Miller said the intro nods to Free's hit "All Right Now."</p>
        <p>It was the band's second Hot 100 number one, and it also reached number one in Canada.</p>
        <p>This site's weekly data shows one week at number one, the chart week of November 6, 1976.</p>
        <p>Chicago's "If You Leave Me Now" was on top before it, and Rod Stewart's "Tonight's the Night (Gonna Be Alright)" came next.</p>
        <p>More of the decade's guitar rock is on the <a href="/blog/genres/70s-rock/">70s rock</a> page.</p>

        <h3>1977: "You Light Up My Life" by Debby Boone</h3>
        <p>Joseph Brooks wrote "You Light Up My Life" for the film of the same name.</p>
        <p>In the film, actress Didi Conn lip-synced to Kasey Cisyk's original recording.</p>
        <p>Wikipedia says Debby Boone sang her vocal over an instrumental track already made for the soundtrack.</p>
        <p>The song won Brooks the Academy Award for Best Original Song and the Grammy for Song of the Year.</p>
        <p>This site's weekly data shows ten weeks at number one, from October 15 to December 17, 1977.</p>
        <p>Meco's "Star Wars Theme/Cantina Band" was on top before it, and the Bee Gees' "How Deep Is Your Love" came next.</p>
        <p>So the chart week that contains November 12 was the fifth of ten.</p>
        <p>The song is number 3 on the 1978 year-end Hot 100, in this site's year-end data.</p>
        <p>More slow songs from the decade are on the <a href="/blog/songs/70s-slow-songs/">70s slow songs</a> list.</p>

        <h3>1978: "MacArthur Park" by Donna Summer</h3>
        <p>Jimmy Webb wrote "MacArthur Park" in 1967, and Richard Harris recorded it first.</p>
        <p>Donna Summer's disco version came out in August 1978.</p>
        <p>It was part of the nearly 18-minute "MacArthur Park Suite" on her double album Live and More.</p>
        <p>Giorgio Moroder and Pete Bellotte produced it.</p>
        <p>Wikipedia says it was her first number one on the Hot 100.</p>
        <p>This site's weekly data shows three weeks at number one, from November 11 to November 25, 1978.</p>
        <p>Anne Murray's "You Needed Me" was on top before it, and Barbra Streisand and Neil Diamond's "You Don't Bring Me Flowers" came next.</p>
        <p>So the chart week that contains November 12 was the first of three.</p>
        <p>The song is number 12 on the 1979 year-end Hot 100, in this site's year-end data.</p>
        <p>The full story is on the <a href="/blog/songs/macarthur-park-donna-summer/">MacArthur Park</a> page.</p>

        <h3>1979: "Heartache Tonight" by Eagles</h3>
        <p>Don Henley, Glenn Frey, Bob Seger and JD Souther wrote "Heartache Tonight."</p>
        <p>Glenn Frey sang lead, and it came from the album The Long Run.</p>
        <p>The single came out on September 18, 1979.</p>
        <p>Wikipedia says it was the band's last song to reach number one on the Hot 100.</p>
        <p>It won the Grammy for Best Rock Performance by a Duo or Group with Vocal.</p>
        <p>This site's weekly data shows one week at number one, the chart week of November 10, 1979.</p>
        <p>M's "Pop Muzik" was on top before it, and the Commodores' "Still" came next.</p>
        <p>The full story is on the <a href="/blog/songs/heartache-tonight/">Heartache Tonight</a> page.</p>

